use std
into pcbsi.amni
dialect js

This file is a library built for the web as a wasm module; its `main()` does nothing. JavaScript drives it through its exported functions (every name starting with ps_ is exported). It computes printed-circuit-board signal and power numbers: the characteristic impedance of traces with a two-dimensional electrostatic field solver over the board cross section (microstrip, stripline and coplanar waveguide with ground, single traces and differential pairs, with an optional solder-mask layer), the trace width that hits a target impedance, IPC-2221 trace current capacity with resistance and voltage drop, and via resistance, current, inductance and capacitance. All lengths are millimeters. Its only import line is `import math;`, ending with a semicolon. Every int used in real math is converted with `as real`, and a real used as an int with `as int`, since Helium has no real or int conversion calls.

```amni
extern fn ps_view_f64(id i32, xs real[:]) = "ps_view_f64";
extern fn ps_view_i32(id i32, xs i32[:]) = "ps_view_i32";
```

Module state. Real `GR` (grid growth ratio between neighboring cells), starting at 1.3. Ints, all starting at 0: `NX` and `NY` (grid line counts across and up), `NR` (conductor rectangles), `NT` (tracks for the width check). Growable f64 arrays: `IN` (48 inputs), `OUT` (48 outputs), `XS` (NX grid x coordinates), `YS` (NY grid y coordinates), `EP` (relative permittivity of each grid cell, (NX minus 1) times (NY minus 1)), `CR` (5 per conductor rectangle: left, right, bottom, top, conductor id), `PHI`, `AXC`, `AYC`, `DG`, `RR`, `ZZ`, `PP`, `QQ`, `MD` (1 per grid node each), `CP` (8 capacitances per meter from the last `solve1`), `TW`, `TL`, `TU` (1 per track: width, smallest allowed width, largest allowed width). Growable i32 arrays: `KN` (1 per grid node: 0 free, 1 first trace, 2 second trace, 3 ground), `TF` (1 per track: 0 fine, 1 too narrow, 2 too wide). Grid node (i, j) has index j times `NX` plus i; grid cell (i, j) between lines i and i plus 1 and lines j and j plus 1 has index j times (`NX` minus 1) plus i.

Inputs in `IN` for the field solver: 0 structure (0 microstrip on an outer layer, 1 stripline between two planes, 2 coplanar waveguide with a ground plane below), 1 pair (above 0.5 for a differential pair), 2 trace width w, 3 edge-to-edge gap s between the two traces of a pair, 4 copper thickness t, 5 dielectric height h1 from the ground plane below to the bottom of the trace, 6 dielectric height h2 from the top of the trace to the plane above (stripline only), 7 relative permittivity er1 of the dielectric below the trace, 8 relative permittivity er2 of the dielectric around and above the trace (stripline only), 9 solder-mask thickness over the trace (0 for none, ignored for stripline), 10 solder-mask relative permittivity, 11 gap g from the trace edges to the coplanar ground copper (structure 2 only), 12 grid quality (1 normal, 2 fine), 13 solver tolerance (0 means 1e-8). Inputs 16 to 22 are for `ps_trace` and 24 to 31 for `ps_via`.

Outputs in `OUT` from the field solver: 0 characteristic impedance Z0 in ohms (for a pair the square root of the odd-mode times the even-mode impedance), 1 effective permittivity, 2 capacitance in picofarads per meter, 3 inductance in nanohenries per meter, 4 propagation delay in picoseconds per millimeter, 5 odd-mode impedance, 6 even-mode impedance, 7 differential impedance (2 times odd-mode), 8 common-mode impedance (even-mode divided by 2), 9 odd-mode effective permittivity, 10 even-mode effective permittivity, 11 coupling coefficient, 12 `NX`, 13 `NY`, 14 total solver iterations, 15 fitted trace width, 16 fit steps, 17 impedance reached by the fit. Outputs 16 to 22 also hold `ps_trace` results and 24 to 29 `ps_via` results, as described there.

Sizing helpers. `zf(xs, n, x)` takes an f64 growable array by reference: xs is emptied, then gets `reserve`(n), then is resized to n entries equal to x. `zi(xs, n, x)` does the same for an i32 growable array, resizing to n entries equal to x as i32. On the web an array that holds entries cannot grow past the room it first reserved, so every array is emptied before it is sized again.

The exported function `ps_views()`, declared with the export keyword, registers the arrays JavaScript reads or writes through ps_view_f64 and ps_view_i32: f64 views 1 `IN`, 2 `OUT`, 3 `XS`, 4 `YS`, 5 `PHI`, 6 `EP`, 7 `TW`, 8 `TL`, 9 `TU`; i32 views 10 `KN`, 11 `TF`. It returns 0.

The exported function `ps_init()`, declared with the export keyword, sizes with the helpers `IN` and `OUT` to 48 zeros, `XS`, `YS`, `PHI` and `EP` to 1 zero, `CR` to 40 zeros, `CP` to 8 zeros, `TW`, `TL` and `TU` to 1 zero, `KN` and `TF` to 1 zero, calls `ps_views` and returns 0.

The exported function `ps_tracks(n)`, declared with the export keyword, takes an i32 track count, sets `NT` to n, sizes with the helpers `TW`, `TL` and `TU` to n zeros (at least 1 entry) and `TF` to n zeros (at least 1 entry), calls `ps_views` and returns n.

The exported function `ps_check()`, declared with the export keyword, sets for each track k below `NT` TF[k] to 1 when TL[k] is above 0.0 and TW[k] is below TL[k] times 0.999, else to 2 when TU[k] is above 0.0 and TW[k] is above TU[k] times 1.001, else to 0, and returns the number of tracks whose TF is not 0.

`seg(a, b, d0, dm, o)` takes four reals and an f64 growable array o by reference, and pushes onto o the grid coordinates that split the span from a to b, ending with b (a itself is already in o). With half equal to b minus a, halved: a local f64 growable array hs starts empty; d is the smaller of d0 and half; s starts at 0.0; while s plus d is at most half and hs has fewer than 4000 entries, d is pushed onto hs, d is added to s and d becomes the smaller of d times `GR` and dm. Then r is half minus s: when hs is empty, half is pushed onto hs; otherwise when r is above 0.5 times the last entry of hs, r is pushed onto hs; otherwise r is added to the last entry of hs. Then x starts at a; for each entry of hs in order, it is added to x and x is pushed onto o; then for each entry of hs in reverse order (last entry first), it is added to x and x is pushed onto o. Finally the last entry of o is set to exactly b. It returns 0.

`sortu(xs, n)` takes an f64 growable array by reference and an i32 count n, sorts its first n entries in ascending order with an insertion sort, then removes, keeping the earlier one, every entry that is within 1e-9 times (1.0 plus the absolute value of the earlier kept entry) of the entry kept before it, compacting the kept entries to the front, and returns the number of kept entries.

`rect(x0, x1, y0, y1, id)` takes five reals, writes them into `CR` at 5 times `NR` to 5 times `NR` plus 4, adds 1 to `NR` and returns 0.

`ps_grid()` builds the cross-section grid from `IN`. kind is IN[0] as int; pair is true when IN[1] is above 0.5; w, s, t, h1, h2, er1, er2 are IN[2] to IN[8]; mk is IN[9] when kind is not 1 and is above 0.0, else 0.0; erm is IN[10]; g is IN[11]; q is the larger of IN[12] and 0.25. span is 2 w plus s for a pair and w otherwise; xa is minus span divided by 2. yt is h1 plus t. hh is h1 plus t plus h2 when kind is 1 and h1 otherwise; ext is 12.0 times the larger of hh and w; cg is g when kind is 2 and 0.0 otherwise; xw is span divided by 2 plus cg plus ext; ytop is h1 plus t plus h2 when kind is 1, and yt plus mk plus ext otherwise. d0x is the smallest of w divided by 8, t divided by 2, s divided by 8 (only for a pair), g divided by 8 (only when kind is 2) and h1 divided by 8, then divided by q; d0y is the smallest of t divided by 4, h1 divided by 8, mk divided by 3 (only when mk is above 0.0) and h2 divided by 8 (only when kind is 1), then divided by q; dm is the larger of hh and w, divided by 2 q. Local f64 arrays bx and by of 16 zeros collect breakpoints, with counts nbx and nby: bx gets minus xw, xw, xa and xa plus w, plus (for a pair) xa plus w plus s and xa plus 2 w plus s, plus (when kind is 2) minus span divided by 2 minus g and span divided by 2 plus g; by gets 0.0, h1, yt and ytop, plus yt plus mk when mk is above 0.0. Then nbx is sortu(bx, nbx) and nby is sortu(by, nby). `XS` is emptied, gets reserve(16384), and bx[0] is pushed; for each k from 0 to nbx minus 2, seg(bx[k], bx[k plus 1], d0x, dm, `XS`). `YS` is built the same way from by with d0y. `NX` and `NY` are the lengths of `XS` and `YS`, and nn is `NX` times `NY`. `NR` is set to 0, then rect(xa, xa plus w, h1, yt, 1.0); for a pair rect(xa plus w plus s, xa plus 2 w plus s, h1, yt, 2.0); when kind is 2, rect(minus xw, minus span divided by 2 minus g, h1, yt, 3.0) and rect(span divided by 2 plus g, xw, h1, yt, 3.0). With the helpers, `KN` is sized to nn zeros and `PHI`, `AXC`, `AYC`, `DG`, `RR`, `ZZ`, `PP` and `QQ` to nn zeros, and `EP` to (`NX` minus 1) times (`NY` minus 1) zeros. With tol equal to 1e-9 times (1.0 plus xw plus ytop): every node (i, j) gets KN 3 when i is 0 or `NX` minus 1 or j is 0 or `NY` minus 1; otherwise the conductor id (as i32) of the first rectangle r below `NR` with CR[5r] minus tol at most XS[i], XS[i] at most CR[5r plus 1] plus tol, CR[5r plus 2] minus tol at most YS[j] and YS[j] at most CR[5r plus 3] plus tol; otherwise 0. Every cell (i, j) with center height yc equal to the mean of YS[j] and YS[j plus 1] gets EP equal to: when kind is 1, er1 when yc is below h1 and er2 otherwise; when kind is not 1, er1 when yc is below h1, else erm when mk is above 0.0 and yc is below yt plus mk, else 1.0. It calls `ps_views` and returns nn.

`coef(air)` takes an i32 air flag and fills the edge coefficients of the box-integration discretization of the divergence of permittivity times the gradient, with e(i, j) the permittivity of cell (i, j): 1.0 when air is not 0, else EP of that cell. For every node (i, j): AXC (the edge from node (i, j) to node (i plus 1, j)) is 0.0 when i is `NX` minus 1, otherwise the sum of e(i, j minus 1) times (YS[j] minus YS[j minus 1]) divided by 2 (only when j is above 0) and e(i, j) times (YS[j plus 1] minus YS[j]) divided by 2 (only when j is below `NY` minus 1), divided by (XS[i plus 1] minus XS[i]). AYC (the edge from node (i, j) to node (i, j plus 1)) is 0.0 when j is `NY` minus 1, otherwise the sum of e(i minus 1, j) times (XS[i] minus XS[i minus 1]) divided by 2 (only when i is above 0) and e(i, j) times (XS[i plus 1] minus XS[i]) divided by 2 (only when i is below `NX` minus 1), divided by (YS[j plus 1] minus YS[j]). Then for every node n, DG[n] is AXC[n] plus AYC[n] plus AXC[n minus 1] (only when n mod `NX` is above 0) plus AYC[n minus `NX`] (only when n is at least `NX`). It returns 0.

`flux(xs, n)` takes an f64 growable array by reference and an i32 node index n of a free node (never on the grid border) and returns AXC[n] times (xs[n plus 1] minus xs[n]) plus AXC[n minus 1] times (xs[n minus 1] minus xs[n]) plus AYC[n] times (xs[n plus `NX`] minus xs[n]) plus AYC[n minus `NX`] times (xs[n minus `NX`] minus xs[n]).

`mic()` fills `MD` with the pivots of a modified incomplete Cholesky factorization of the grid matrix, whose diagonal is DG and whose couplings are AXC to the right neighbor and AYC to the upper neighbor. `MD` is sized with the helper to `NX` times `NY` entries equal to 1.0. For every free node n (KN[n] is 0) in increasing order of n: e starts at DG[n]. With l equal to n minus 1, when KN[l] is 0: b is AXC[l], c is AYC[l] when KN[l plus `NX`] is 0 and 0.0 otherwise, and e decreases by (b times b plus 0.99 times b times c) divided by MD[l]. With m equal to n minus `NX`, when KN[m] is 0: c is AYC[m], b is AXC[m] when KN[m plus 1] is 0 and 0.0 otherwise, and e decreases by (c times c plus 0.99 times c times b) divided by MD[m]. When e is below 0.01 times DG[n], e becomes DG[n]. MD[n] is e. It returns 0.

`prec()` applies the factored preconditioner to the residual `RR` and writes the result into `ZZ`. Forward pass, for n from 0 up to `NX` times `NY` minus 1 in increasing order: when KN[n] is 0, ZZ[n] is (RR[n] plus AXC[n minus 1] times ZZ[n minus 1] plus AYC[n minus `NX`] times ZZ[n minus `NX`]) divided by MD[n]; otherwise ZZ[n] is 0.0. Backward pass, for n from `NX` times `NY` minus 1 down to 0 in decreasing order: when KN[n] is 0, ZZ[n] becomes ZZ[n] plus (AXC[n] times ZZ[n plus 1] plus AYC[n] times ZZ[n plus `NX`]) divided by MD[n]. It returns 0.

`cg(va, vb)` takes two reals and solves for the potential with conjugate gradients preconditioned by `prec`. First every node with KN 1 gets PHI va, KN 2 gets PHI vb and KN 3 gets PHI 0.0; free nodes keep their PHI as the starting guess. Then it calls `mic()`. tol is IN[13] when it is above 0.0 and 1e-8 otherwise. bn is the square root of the sum over free nodes n of the square of the fixed-neighbor part of the flux, which is the sum of the coefficient times the neighbor PHI over only those of the four neighbors whose KN is not 0 (using the same coefficients as `flux`); when bn is 0.0 it returns 0. For every node n: RR[n] is flux(`PHI`, n) when KN[n] is 0 and 0.0 otherwise. It calls `prec()`; then for every node n PP[n] is ZZ[n], and rz is the sum of RR times ZZ over all nodes. For it from 1 to 30000: for every free node n, QQ[n] is minus flux(`PP`, n), which equals DG[n] times PP[n] minus (AXC[n] times PP[n plus 1] plus AXC[n minus 1] times PP[n minus 1] plus AYC[n] times PP[n plus `NX`] plus AYC[n minus `NX`] times PP[n minus `NX`]), and QQ is 0.0 at fixed nodes; pq is the sum of PP times QQ; when pq is at most 0.0 it returns it; al is rz divided by pq; every free node gets PHI plus al times PP and RR minus al times QQ; when the square root of the sum of RR squared is at most tol times bn it returns it; it calls `prec()`; rzn is the sum of RR times ZZ over all nodes; be is rzn divided by rz; rz becomes rzn; PP becomes ZZ plus be times PP at free nodes. After the loop it returns 30000.

`energy()` returns the sum over every node n of AXC[n] times the square of (PHI[n plus 1] minus PHI[n]) (only when n mod `NX` is below `NX` minus 1) plus AYC[n] times the square of (PHI[n plus `NX`] minus PHI[n]) (only when n is below (`NY` minus 1) times `NX`).

`solve1(gr)` takes a real grid growth ratio, sets `GR` to gr, calls `ps_grid()`, solves the cross section and writes capacitances per meter into `CP`; e0 is 8.8541878128e-12 and it returns the total of the iteration counts that `cg` returns. For a single trace (IN[1] at most 0.5): coef(1), cg(1.0, 0.0), CP[1] is e0 times energy(); coef(0), cg(1.0, 0.0), CP[0] is e0 times energy(). For a pair: coef(1), cg(1.0, 1.0), CP[3] is e0 times energy() divided by 2; coef(0), cg(1.0, 1.0), CP[2] is e0 times energy() divided by 2; then every PHI is set to 0.0; coef(1), cg(1.0, minus 1.0), CP[5] is e0 times energy() divided by 2; coef(0), cg(1.0, minus 1.0), CP[4] is e0 times energy() divided by 2.

The exported function `ps_solve()`, declared with the export keyword, solves on two grids and extrapolates, because the grid error falls in proportion to the growth ratio minus 1. q is the larger of IN[12] and 0.25. it is solve1(1.0 plus 0.3 divided by q); a local f64 array c1 holds a copy of CP[0] to CP[5]; it grows by solve1(1.0 plus 0.15 divided by q); then for k from 0 to 5, CP[k] becomes 2.0 times CP[k] minus c1[k]. c is 299792458.0. For a single trace (IN[1] at most 0.5): cc is CP[0] and ca is CP[1]; OUT[0] is 1.0 divided by (c times the square root of cc times ca), OUT[1] is cc divided by ca, OUT[2] is cc times 1e12, OUT[3] is 1e9 divided by (c times c times ca), OUT[4] is the square root of (cc divided by ca) divided by c, times 1e9, and OUT[5] to OUT[11] are 0.0. For a pair: ce is CP[2], cea is CP[3], co is CP[4] and coa is CP[5]; zo is 1.0 divided by (c times the square root of co times coa) and ze is 1.0 divided by (c times the square root of ce times cea). OUT[5] is zo, OUT[6] is ze, OUT[7] is 2 zo, OUT[8] is ze divided by 2, OUT[9] is co divided by coa, OUT[10] is ce divided by cea, OUT[11] is (ze minus zo) divided by (ze plus zo), OUT[0] is the square root of zo times ze, OUT[1] is the mean of OUT[9] and OUT[10], OUT[2] is co times 1e12, OUT[3] is 1e9 divided by (c times c times coa) and OUT[4] is the square root of (co divided by coa) divided by c, times 1e9. In both cases OUT[12] is `NX` as real, OUT[13] is `NY` as real (of the finer grid) and OUT[14] is it as real. It returns it.

The exported function `ps_fit(target, which)`, declared with the export keyword, takes a real target impedance and an i32 which (0 to match OUT[0], 1 to match the differential impedance OUT[7]) and changes the trace width IN[2] until the solved impedance is within 0.1 percent of the target, solving with `ps_solve` and reading the matched output z after each solve. w0 is IN[2] and z0 the result for it. When the absolute value of z0 divided by target minus 1.0 is below 0.001, it stops. Otherwise w1 is w0 times (z0 divided by target) raised to the power 1.0 divided by 0.7, kept between w0 divided by 8 and w0 times 8. Up to 12 times: IN[2] becomes w1 and z1 is the result; it stops when the absolute value of z1 divided by target minus 1.0 is below 0.001; sl is (the natural log of z1 minus the natural log of z0) divided by (the natural log of w1 minus the natural log of w0), replaced by minus 0.7 when it is above minus 0.001 or when w1 equals w0; w2 is the exponential of (the natural log of w1 plus (the natural log of target minus the natural log of z1) divided by sl), kept between w1 divided by 8 and w1 times 8; then w0 becomes w1, z0 becomes z1 and w1 becomes w2. At the end IN[2] holds the last width solved, OUT[15] is that width, OUT[16] is the number of solves as real and OUT[17] the last matched impedance. It returns the number of solves.

The exported function `ps_trace()`, declared with the export keyword, computes IPC-2221 trace numbers from `IN`: w is IN[16] (width), t is IN[17] (copper thickness), dt is IN[18] (allowed temperature rise in degrees C, at least 0.1), k is 0.024 when IN[19] is above 0.5 (inner layer) and 0.048 otherwise, len is IN[20] (trace length), cur is IN[21] (current in amperes), ta is IN[22] (ambient temperature in degrees C). The cross section in square mils is a equal to w times t divided by 0.00064516. OUT[16] is the current capacity k times dt raised to 0.44 times a raised to 0.725; OUT[17] is the width needed for cur at dt, which is (cur divided by (k times dt raised to 0.44)) raised to the power 1.0 divided by 0.725, times 0.00064516, divided by t; OUT[18] is the temperature rise at cur, (cur divided by (k times a raised to 0.725)) raised to the power 1.0 divided by 0.44 (0.0 when a is at most 0.0); OUT[19] is the resistance in ohms 1.724e-8 times (1.0 plus 0.00393 times (ta plus OUT[18] minus 20.0)) times len divided by (w times t), times 1000.0; OUT[20] is cur times OUT[19]; OUT[21] is cur times cur times OUT[19]; OUT[22] is w times t. It returns 0.

The exported function `ps_via()`, declared with the export keyword, computes via numbers from `IN`: d is IN[24] (drill diameter), p is IN[25] (barrel plating thickness), h is IN[26] (via length, the board thickness), pd is IN[27] (pad diameter), ad is IN[28] (anti-pad diameter in planes), er is IN[29], cur is IN[30] (current in amperes), dt is IN[31] (allowed temperature rise, at least 0.1). The barrel cross section is a equal to pi times p times (d plus p), and am is a divided by 0.00064516 (square mils). OUT[24] is the barrel resistance 1.724e-8 times h divided by a, times 1000.0; OUT[25] is the current capacity 0.048 times dt raised to 0.44 times am raised to 0.725; OUT[26] is the temperature rise at cur, (cur divided by (0.048 times am raised to 0.725)) raised to the power 1.0 divided by 0.44; OUT[27] is the inductance in nanohenries 0.2 times h times (the natural log of 4 h divided by d, plus 1.0); OUT[28] is the capacitance in picofarads 1.41 times er times h divided by 25.4 times pd divided by (ad minus pd), or 0.0 when ad is at most pd; OUT[29] is the via impedance, the square root of OUT[27] divided by OUT[28] times 31.6227766 when OUT[28] is above 0.0, else 0.0. It returns 0.

`main()` does nothing.
